{
 "measured": "2026-10-12, production build (next build, next start on localhost:3300), headless Chrome over the DevTools protocol. Not Lighthouse, not a real device, not Safari, not guests.",
 "method": {
  "lcp": "PerformanceObserver largest-contentful-paint, median of 3 fresh loads, cache disabled",
  "layoutShift": "sum of layout-shift entries without recent input, at load and after a real scroll to the foot of the page",
  "throttling": "slow 4G: 150 ms latency, 1.6 Mbps down, 750 kbps up, 4x CPU",
  "clipBytes": "Network.dataReceived for the .mp4, by time since navigation"
 },
 "caveats": [
  "Every picture is a drawn stand-in of about 4 KB and the clip is made from three of them. LCP with stand-ins says little about LCP with photographs, and motion quality is unverified.",
  "From 768px Chrome reports the clip’s first frame as the LCP element, because the stand-in poster has too few bytes for its area to count.",
  "photographWeightPoster replaces only the poster with a synthetic 188 KB JPEG sent through the throttled network: a sensitivity test, not VNest photography.",
  "The comparison rows for B+, B original, A and the current page were measured the same day on the build before the fallback-typeface fix, which changed only the Living Frames route."
 ],
